Find Many Strings
=================

"Find Many Strings" is an extension for the Chromium (and Chrome) browser. It tries to highlight the word(s) searched for in a webpage. Searching a new string will not remove the highlights done for the previously searched string(s).

The extension is open-sourced and licensed under the MIT License. So you can use the extensions without feeling paranoid :-)

The icon for this extension is the Tamil word "தேடு" which means "Search". I used the awesome Gimp project to create this icon.

After a search, the popup lists each search string in its highlight color with ▲ ▼ buttons and a "current / total" counter, so you can step through the matches of each string separately. The page scrolls to the selected match and outlines it. Reopening the popup on the same page brings the list back.

Highlight colors come from a fixed set of eight light colors with dark text, chosen to stay readable on both light and dark websites. A string keeps its color across searches on the same page until the highlights are cleared. The popup follows the browser's light or dark theme.

Screencast of the extension http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IgB0_P5pPz4

Privacy: the extension uses only the "activeTab" and "scripting" permissions. It can read or change a page only after you click its toolbar icon on that page, and it sends no data anywhere.

Installing from source
======================

1. Open chrome://extensions and turn on "Developer mode".
2. Click "Load unpacked" and select this directory.


External References
===================

All the source code of this extension are written by Sankar P <sankar.curiosity@gmail.com> and Varunkumar Nagarajan <varunkumar.n@gmail.com>, except the two js files mentioned below, which we copied and used.

	a) jquery.js: jQuery 4.1.0, the official minified build from https://code.jquery.com/jquery-4.1.0.min.js

	b) jquery.highlight-3.js: This is written by Johann Burkard - http://johannburkard.de/blog/programming/javascript/highlight-javascript-text-higlighting-jquery-plugin.html     I have used this for walking through the DOM tree. Thanks Johann :-)



